EXT. RICE TERRACE BUNKER - DAWN

Mud. Standing water, ankle deep. Sandbags slumped like drunks. A shell crater full of rain.

LIEUTENANT NIKOLA VASKO, 30s, helmet gone, one sleeve black with blood, sits against the sandbags. Staring at nothing.

CORPORAL ABEBE TESFAYE, 20s, kneels beside him. Rifle across his knees. Hands shaking.

The guns have stopped. That is the horror of it.

ABEBE
Sir. Sir, it's quiet.

VASKO
I hear.

ABEBE
Should we go?

VASKO
Where?

Abebe has no answer. A bird starts up in the flooded trees. Another.

ABEBE
They're singing.

VASKO
They don't know.

Abebe unslings his canteen. Offers it. Vasko drinks. Hands it back.

VASKO (CONT'D)
How many?

ABEBE
Two of us.

VASKO
Three. Count the radio man.

ABEBE
He's not -- sir. He's not counting.

Vasko closes his eyes. Nods.

Boots in the mud. Abebe snaps his rifle up.

LINNEA STROM, 30s, a red cross on her armband, comes over the dike with a canvas bag. She is soaked to the thigh. She raises both hands slowly.

LINNEA
Medic. Don't.

Abebe lowers the rifle an inch.

ABEBE
Which side?

LINNEA
Yours. I think.

She kneels at Vasko. Cuts the sleeve with scissors. He doesn't look.

LINNEA (CONT'D)
Lieutenant. Stay with me.

VASKO
I'm here.

LINNEA
Good. Keep being here.

Abebe sits. The rifle slides into the water. He lets it.

ABEBE
Is it over?

LINNEA
For today.

She pulls a bandage tight. Vasko hisses.

The sun clears the ridge. Light spills across the flooded terrace, gold on grey.

Nobody speaks. Birds go on singing.
